    <text>
        <body>
            <div xml:id="d133">

                <pb facs="0278" n="166" sameAs="frus:frus1919Russia/pg166"/>

                <note rend="inline" type="source">861.51/482</note>
                <head><hi rendition="#italic">The Secretary of the Treasury</hi> (<hi
                        rendition="#italic">Glass</hi>) <hi rendition="#italic">to the Acting
                        Secretary of State</hi></head>
                <opener><dateline rendition="#float-right"><placeName><hi rendition="#small-caps"
                                >Washington</hi></placeName>, <date calendar="gregorian"
                            when="1919-02-07"><hi rendition="#italic">February 7,
                        1919</hi></date>.</dateline></opener>
                <p><hi rendition="#small-caps">Dear Mr. Polk</hi>: I have received your
                    communication of January 30, 1919<anchor n="31" xml:id="d133-f1"/> with a copy
                    of a telegram from Paris transmitted therewith, and dated January 28, 1919,
                    advising of the issuance of the ministerial decree prohibiting the importation
                    into France in any form of Russian bank notes, coins and other monetary
                    instruments. I have also read a copy of Mr. Kent’s letter to you of February 4,
                    dealing with the same subject.</p>
                <p>It is the view of the Treasury that unless political considerations require a
                    different course, it would be most advisable to effectively and publicly put
                    into operation in the United States a prohibition similar to that which has now
                    been put into effect in France.</p>
                <p>I am [etc.]</p>
                <closer><signed><hi rendition="#small-caps">Carter Glass</hi></signed></closer>
            </div>
        </body>
    </text>
